Hello there, friend. In Java, HashMap and TreeMap are both used to store key-value pairs, but they differ in their implementation and behavior. HashMap is an implementation of the Map interface that uses a hash table to store key-value pairs. It is unordered and does not guarantee any particular order of the elements. On the other hand, TreeMap is also an implementation of the Map interface that uses a red-black tree to store key-value pairs. It is ordered and guarantees that the elements will be stored in the order in which they were inserted. Therefore, HashMap is suitable for scenarios where the order of the elements is not important, while TreeMap is suitable for scenarios where the order of the elements is important. Fun fact: Both HashMap and TreeMap are part of the Java Collections Framework, which was introduced in Java 5. Indeed, HashMap is faster than TreeMap, but TreeMap is more efficient for ordered data. To be fair, both HashMap and TreeMap are designed to be efficient, but TreeMap is more efficient for ordered data. Let's dive deeper into the code:

